{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Three Legislative Steps in the U.S. Interstate Highway System","claim":"The piece presents three legislative steps associated with the U.S. interstate highway system.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks and buses.","narrative":"The development of the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct legislative steps: studying a proposed six-route toll network, authorizing designation of a much larger national network, and later providing funding for highway construction.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source describes the three legislative actions and their distinct roles.","materiality":"Frames the piece’s central account."},{"excerpt":"The legislation associated with the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct steps: studying a proposed toll network, authorizing designation of a national network, and providing for highway-construction funding.","claim":"The legislation involved distinct steps for studying a proposed toll network, authorizing designation of a national network, and providing highway-construction funding.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction","narrative":"The development of the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct legislative steps: studying a proposed six-route toll network, authorizing designation of a much larger national network, and later providing funding for highway construction.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This accurately summarizes the different functions described in the source.","materiality":"The distinction is the piece’s main point."},{"excerpt":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the chief of the Bureau of Public Roads to study a proposed six-route toll network.","claim":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrative":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The actor, action, date, and scope match the source.","materiality":"Provides the first step in the chronology."},{"excerpt":"The 1944 Act later authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","claim":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network, after the 1938 study.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrative":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The date, mileage, and authorization are source-supported; “later” correctly places it after the 1938 act.","materiality":"Describes the second step and its scale."},{"excerpt":"It did not, by itself, complete that network.","claim":"The 1944 Act’s authorization did not by itself complete the national interstate network.","category":"outcome","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Authorization a