es’ identification.","materiality":"Attributes the association with establishing the system to the National Archives."},{"excerpt":"The three legislative steps had different functions: feasibility study, network designation, and construction financing—not a single act of completion.","claim":"The three steps correspond to feasibility study, network designation, and construction financing, and do not amount to a single act of completion.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction. Authorization and designation are not completion.","narrative":"The sequence distinguishes feasibility study, network designation, and a mechanism for construction financing; the National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This closing summary accurately maps the three functions and preserves the distinction between authorization or financing and completion.","materiality":"Reinforces the piece’s central distinction."}]}

